 ],
 "literal": "Having raised the bull as victorious, fire-resembling shining matted hair, / unwardable battle-axe, sapphire-blue throat-possessor; / ocean-grown twisted conch-resembling body, / strong fierce plow palmyra-flag possessor; / polished beautiful gem-resembling body, / sky-rising bird-flag victorious possessor; / sapphire-like peacock-raised unfailing victory / peacock-mount bright red-one; thus / world-protecting Yama-like strength / undefeated good fame four-persons among; / Yama you resemble in unwardable anger; / strength you resemble of the white-one; / fame you resemble of the destroyer of those who despise; / Murugan you resemble in accomplishing what was intended; / there and there, to each of them, because of resembling, everywhere / rare things are there to you indeed? Therefore, / to suppliants precious ornaments without diminishing giving, / Yavanas' good ships brought cool fragrant wine, / gold-made decorated vessel holding, daily, / bright-bangled women serving, joy excelling, / thus sweetly may you live, O mighty-sword Maṟaṉ! / Beautiful expanse sky's dense darkness removing / hot-rayed lord like, and west direction's / cool-rayed moon like, / having stood, may you endure with the world together indeed.",
 "translation": "He of the shining matted hair resembling fire, who raises the victorious bull on his flag, wields the unyielding battle-axe, and has a sapphire-blue throat; he whose body resembles a twisted conch grown in the ocean, who holds the strong, fierce plow, and has the palmyra flag; he whose body resembles a polished beautiful gem, who has the victorious bird flag rising to the sky; and he of unfailing victory who raises the sapphire-like peacock, riding the peacock mount, the bright red one—among these four of undefeated good fame and Yama-like strength who protect the world: you resemble Yama in your unyielding anger; you resemble the white one (Balarama) in strength; you resemble the destroyer of enemies (Krishna) in fame; and you resemble Murugan in accomplishing whatever you intend. Since you resemble each of them in their respective qualities, is there anything in this world that is difficult for you to achieve? Therefore, giving precious ornaments to suppliants without limit, holding the cool, fragrant wine brought by the good ships of the Yavanas in decorated gold vessels, served daily by bright-bangled women, may you live in great joy, O Maṟaṉ of the mighty sword! Like the hot-rayed lord (the Sun) who removes the dense darkness of the beautiful sky, and like the cool-rayed moon of the west, may you endure forever along with this world!",
 "modern": "வெற்றி எருதை உயர்த்திய, நெருப்புப் போன்ற ஒளிரும் சடையையும், மாற்ற முடியாத மழுவையும், நீல நிறக் கழுத்தையும் உடைய சிவன்; கடல் சங்கு போன்ற வெண்மையான உடலையும், கலப்பையையும், பனைக்கொடியையும் உடைய பலராமன்; கழுவப்பட்ட நீலமணி போன்ற உடலையும், கருடக்கொடியையும் உடைய திருமால்; நீல மயில் கொடியையும், பிணிமுகம் என்னும் வாகனத்தையும் உடைய செவ்வேள் ஆகிய நால்வருள்: நீ சினத்தில் சிவனை ஒக்கின்றாய்; வலிமையில் பலராமனை ஒக்கின்றாய்; புகழில் திருமாலை ஒக்கின்றாய்; எண்ணியதை முடிப்பதில் முருகனை ஒக்கின்றாய். இவ்வாறு நால்வரையும் நீ ஒத்திருப்பதால் உனக்கு அரியது என்று ஏதேனும் உண்டோ? இல்லை. அதனால், இரவலர்க்கு அணிகலன்களைக் குறைவின்றி வழங்கி, யவனர் கலங்களில் கொண்டுவந்த குளிர்ந்த நறுமண மதுவை, மகளிர் பொற்கலங்களில் ஏந்தி முகந்து தர, நாள்தோறும் மகிழ்ந்து இனிது வாழ்வாயாக, வாள் ஏந்திய மாறனே! வானின் இருளை அகற்றும் ஞாயிறு போலவும், திங்கள் போலவும், இவ்வுலகத்தோடு நீ நிலைபெற்று வாழ்வாயாக!",
 "explanation": "This poem belongs to the Pāṭāṇ tiṇai and the Pūvai nilai tuṟai, where a mortal king is compared to great deities. The poet Nakkīraṉār addresses the Pandyan king Ilavantikaip Paḷḷit Tuñciya Naṉmāṟaṉ, praising his extraordinary qualities by comparing him to the four great guardian gods of the cosmos: Shiva, Balarama, Krishna/Vishnu, and Murugan. The king is said to possess Shiva's fierce anger, Balarama's physical strength, Krishna's fame, and Murugan's determination in achieving his goals. The poet then describes the king's luxurious court, where he generously rewards suppliants and drinks imported Yavana wine served in golden cups by beautiful women. Finally, the poet blesses the king to live as long as the sun and the moon, enduring alongside the world itself.",
